**19.13. Connection With Hermite Polynomials and Functions**

When \(n\) is a non-negative integer

**19.13.1**: \[U(-n{-}\frac{1}{2},x){=}e^{-1b^{2}}H\!e_{n}(x){=}2^{-1b}e^{-1b^{2}}H\!\left(x/\sqrt{2}\right)\]
**19.13.2**: \[V(n{+}\frac{1}{2},x){=}\sqrt{2/\pi}e^{1b^{2}}H\!e_{n}^{*}(x){=}2^{-1b}e^{b^{2}}H\!\left(x/\sqrt{2}\right)\]

in which \(H\!\left(x\right)\) and \(H\!e_{n}(x)\) are Hermite polynomials (see chapter 22) while

**19.13.3**: \(H\!\left(x\right){=}e^{-\frac{1}{2}x^{2}}\frac{d^{n}}{dx^{n}}\;e^{1b^{2}}{=}(-i)^{n}H\!e_{n}(ix)\)
**19.13.4**: \(H\!\left(x\right){=}e^{-\frac{x^{2}}{2}}\frac{d^{n}}{dx^{n}}\;e^{x^{2}}{=}(-i)^{n}H\!\left( ix\right)\)

This gives one elementary solution to 19.1.2 whenever \(2a\) is an odd integer, positive or negative.
